## 3. Core Competency Frameworks: ABMS, ACGME, and ABIM

### 3.1 ABMS/ACGME Six Core Competencies

The **American Board of Medical Specialties (ABMS)** and the **Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME)** jointly define six core competencies that serve as the competency-based foundation for U.S. graduate medical education and board certification:

| # | Competency Domain | Description | Alignment to This Project |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Medical Knowledge** | Established and evolving biomedical, clinical, and cognate sciences and their application to patient care | Contextual background for clinical reasoning labels (`cr_`) |
| 2 | **Patient Care & Procedural Skills** | Patient-centered, compassionate, appropriate, and effective treatment of health problems, including patient-centered decision-making | Direct target via SHARE labels (`sh_`), exam labels (`exam_`), and outcome covariates |
| 3 | **Practice-Based Learning & Improvement** | Investigation and evaluation of patient care practices, appraisal and assimilation of scientific evidence, and improvements in practice | Supported through training recency covariates and longitudinal tracking |
| 4 | **Systems-Based Practice** | Awareness of and responsiveness to the larger context and system of healthcare | Captured via compliance/risk labels, utilization outcomes |
| 5 | **Interpersonal & Communication Skills** | Effective information exchange and teaming with patients, families, and health professionals | **Primary target** — Calgary, NURSE, SPIKES, McLaughlin labels |
| 6 | **Professionalism** | Commitment to carrying out professional responsibilities, adherence to ethical principles, sensitivity to diversity, humanism | Captured via NURSE empathy labels, professionalism covariates |

Official ABMS standards for Initial Certification require ABMS Member Boards to incorporate all six competencies, with annotation text defining each domain with explicit references to patient-centered decision-making, effective listening, nonverbal communication, health literacy, ethics, and humanism.

**Source**: [ABMS Standards for Initial Certification](https://www.abms.org/board-certification/board-certification-standards/standards-for-initial-certification/)

### 3.2 ACGME Milestones System

ACGME Milestones operationalize the six competency domains into **specialty-specific, developmental "narrative descriptors"** arranged along five levels (Levels 1–5):

- **Purpose**: Formative assessment, shared language across programs, and program improvement — *not* externally imposed high-stakes decision tools.
- **Structure**: Each specialty has its own Milestones document with subcompetencies mapped to the six core domains, each described through behavioral/narrative descriptors at progressive developmental levels.
- **Governance caution**: ACGME has explicitly stated that individual Milestones data "were not designed or intended" for external high-stakes decisions (e.g., licensing, credentialing), warning that such use can create negative incentives and potential rating inflation.

This governance caution is **directly relevant**: any automated NLP-derived "grades" or behavioral assessments must be positioned as formative or decision-support unless specifically validated for a high-stakes context under rigorous governance.

**Sources**:
- [ACGME-I Milestones Overview](https://www.acgme-i.org/accreditation-process/milestones/)
- [ACGME Position on High-Stakes Use (PDF)](https://www.acgme.org/globalassets/PDFs/Milestones/UseofIndividualMilestonesDatabyExternalEntitiesforHighStakesDecisions.pdf)

### 3.3 ABIM's Role and Milestones Context

The **American Board of Internal Medicine (ABIM)** describes Milestones as **outcomes-based expectations** within the six domains, co-convened by ACGME/ABIM/AAIM working groups, used for normative data, program improvement, and demonstrating GME accountability.

**Source**: [ABIM Milestones](https://www.abim.org/program-directors-administrators/milestones)

### 3.4 ACGME Milestones 2.0 Developmental Trajectory

The Milestones 2.0 framework maps clinical communication skills across a developmental continuum that directly informs how behavioral labels should be interpreted and scored:

| Level | Stage | Description | Framework Alignment |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Level 1** | Novice | Rule-driven. Relies on checklists for basic introductions. | CCG Phase I basics |
| **Level 2** | Advanced Beginner | Begins to establish therapeutic relationships in routine situations; identifies emotional cues. | NURSE Naming; basic rapport |
| **Level 3** | Competent | Integrates I.C.E. and NURSE's exploring steps to align goals with treatment; utilizes basic shared decision-making. | CCG Phase II + NURSE Explore + basic SHARE |
| **Level 4** | Proficient | Uses "dual process thinking" and independently manages complex situations; utilizes the full SHARE approach even amidst conflict. | Full SHARE; SPIKES mastery; complex SDM |
| **Level 5** | Expert | Acts as a role model/coach; seamlessly adapts frameworks to atypical environments; manages systems-level ethical issues. | Coaching others; barrier mitigation modeling |

This developmental trajectory provides the **scoring anchor** for the pipeline's "final grades" — each behavioral label detection can be mapped to the appropriate milestone level based on the complexity and sophistication of the observed behavior.

